ding:.6rem}.tier:first-child{background:#e8f2fa}.tier:last-child{background:#e8f4e9}.choices{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.6rem}.choices label{border:1px solid #7e9daa;padding:.5rem;border-radius:.35rem;flex:1 1 12rem}.small{font-size:.92rem}.stage p,.stage li{max-width:78ch}.vocab{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.4rem}.vocab>div{min-width:0}.diagram{max-width:600px;margin:1rem auto}.diagram svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.diagram figcaption{font-size:.9rem}.diagram svg text{font:700 16px Arial,sans-serif;fill:#16323b}.results{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;max-width:36rem}.results th,.results td{border:1px solid #617d85;padding:.25rem;text-align:left}.results td{height:2rem}[data-mbm-guide]{display:none}footer{font-size:.9rem}.calm .hero{background:#415965}.calm .stage{border-left-color:#607680}@media(max-width:600px){header,main,footer{padding:.7rem}.toolbar{padding:.6rem}.tier-grid,.vocab{grid-template-columns:1fr}.choices label{flex-basis:100%}}@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){*{animation:none!important;transition:none!important}}@media print{@page{size:A4;margin:12mm}body{background:#fff;color:#000;font-size:10pt}.hero{background:#fff;color:#000}.hero a,.toolbar,.controls,.skip,[data-mbm-guide]{display:none}.badge{color:#000;padding:0}.stage{border:0;border-top:1px solid #333;border-radius:0;margin:.15rem 0;padding:.35rem 0;break-inside:avoid}.tier-grid{display:block}.tier{border:0;padding:0}.tier h3{display:inline}.tier h3:after{content:': '}.tier p{display:inline}.tier:after{content:'';display:block}h2{font-size:12pt}.words,.model,.support,.stretch,.answer-box,.activity{padding:.25rem;background:#fff;border:0}}</style></head><body><header class="hero"><a href="../../../index.html">← Lessons</a><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to lesson</a><span class="badge">GROW · Science · Autumn 2 · Week 1</span><h1>Why day turns to night</h1><p><strong>I am learning to:</strong> Show why day turns to night and why the Sun seems to move.</p><p class="small">40 minutes · three resets</p><p data-mbm-guide>SoW objective: Explain day and night and the apparent movement of the Sun.</p></header><nav class="toolbar" aria-label="Lesson stages"><button type="button" id="calm" aria-pressed="false">Calm view</button><a href="#arrival">Arrival</a><a href="#wedo">We do</a><a href="#wedo2">We do 2</a><a href="#independent">Independent</a><a href="#exit">Exit</a></nav><main id="main"><section class="stage" id="arrival"><h2>Arrival · 5 min</h2><p>Place a dot on the ball. Point to the side lit by the torch.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Offer a seat or standing spot. End with a one-minute reset.</p></section><section class="stage" id="starter"><h2>Starter · 3 min</h2><p>Think back. Say, point or write one answer for each question.</p><h3>Last time · earlier · last term</h3><ol><li>What goes around Earth?</li><li>Name one mechanism from our forces work.</li><li>Name one force from our first science week.</li></ol><h3>How I know</h3><ul><li>I can point to the day and night sides.</li><li>I can turn the model and explain when night comes.</li><li>I can explain why the Sun seems to move.</li></ul><p data-mbm-guide>Ask three low-stakes questions without a public test.</p></section><section class="stage" id="ido"><h2>I do · 5 min</h2><p>Watch: the torch stays still. I turn the ball. The dot has day when it faces the light. It has night when it faces away.</p><figure class="diagram"><svg id="day-night-diagram" viewBox="0 0 600 260" role="img" aria-label="Simple model: light from the Sun reaches the left side of Earth. A dot on that side has day; a dot away from light has night. The line through Earth is its axis."><title>Day and night as Earth turns</title><defs><clipPath id="earthClip"><circle cx="385" cy="125" r="82"/></clipPath><marker id="tip" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L9 4.5 L0 9" fill="#bd7b0a"/></marker></defs><circle cx="74" cy="124" r="50" fill="#f6cc42" stroke="#66510c" stroke-width="3"/><text x="49" y="130">Sun</text><path d="M135 83 L285 83 M135 125 L285 125 M135 167 L285 167" stroke="#bd7b0a" stroke-width="5" marker-end="url(#tip)"/><text x="196" y="69">light</text><circle cx="385" cy="125" r="82" fill="#b1d8e2" stroke="#174b63" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="385" y="38" width="86" height="176" fill="#485c75" clip-path="url(#earthClip)"/><line x1="385" y1="24" x2="385" y2="226" stroke="#224756" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="8 5"/><circle cx="328" cy="125" r="7" fill="#a8312a"/><circle cx="442" cy="125" r="7" fill="#fff" stroke="#a8312a" stroke-width="3"/><text x="298" y="104">day</text><text x="435" y="104">night</text><text x="393" y="29">axis</text><text x="361" y="239">Earth</text></svg><figcaption>Light reaches one side of Earth. This model is simplified and not to scale.</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><strong>Words</strong><div class="vocab"><div><strong>rotation</strong>: one turn around an axis<br><span class="small">Earth’s rotation brings day and night.</span></div><div><strong>axis</strong>: an imagined line a planet spins around<br><span class="small">Earth turns around its axis.</span></div><div><strong>shadow</strong>: an area where an object blocks light<br><span class="small">The ball makes a shadow on the desk.</span></div><div><strong>orbit</strong>: a curved path around another object<br><span class="small">Earth’s orbit takes it around the Sun.</span></div></div></div><div class="model"><strong>Full model:</strong> Torch still → dot faces the torch: day → turn the ball → dot faces away: night. Earth’s rotation makes the Sun seem to move.</div><div class="answer-box"><strong>Show me:</strong> Show me: where is the dot when that place has day? Point or use a card.</div><p data-mbm-guide>Model the full sequence. Keep light out of eyes. Ask the show-me check.</p></section><section class="stage" id="wedo"><h2>We do · 7 min</h2><p>Now try the same model. Two steps are done. Complete the last step: turn the dot away and name what comes next.</p><div class="support"><strong>Try the model</strong><p>Predict: if the dot turns away from the torch, will it have day or night?</p><p><strong>Kit:</strong> torch, ball or globe, removable dot. Aim light at the ball, away from faces.</p><ol><li>Place the dot on the ball.</li><li>Keep the torch still.</li><li>Turn the dot to face the torch. Record day or night.</li><li>Turn the dot away. Record day or night.</li></ol><table class="results"><thead><tr><th>Dot faces</th><th>Day or night?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torch</td><td><input type="text" aria-label="Day or night when dot faces the torch" size="10"></td></tr><tr><td>Away</td><td><input type="text" aria-label="Day or night when dot faces away" size="10"></td></tr></tbody></table><p data-mbm-guide>Check against the school risk assessment and CLEAPSS guidance. Do not shine the torch into eyes.</p></div><div class="model"><strong>Part-done model:</strong> Torch still → dot faces the torch: day → turn the ball → dot faces away: ____.</div><div class="activity"><label for="faded-choice">The missing word is </label><select id="faded-choice"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="day">day</option><option value="night">night</option></select> <button type="button" id="faded-check">Check</button><p id="faded-feedback" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></div><div class="tier-grid"><div class="tier"><h3>F · Support</h3><p>Point to the torch and dot before naming the missing step.</p></div><div class="tier"><h3>M · Link</h3><p>Fill the blank and explain why.</p></div><div class="tier"><h3>H · Stretch</h3><p>Explain why the torch need not move to change the view at the dot.</p></div></div><p data-mbm-guide>Wait for the missing step. End with a one-minute reset.</p></section><section class="stage" id="wedo2"><h2>We do 2 · 5 min</h2><p>Turn the ball. Choose where the dot is when it is night. Show your choice with the group.</p><div class="answer-box"><h3>Everyone answers</h3><p>It is night at the dot. Where is the dot?</p><div class="choices" role="group" aria-label="Hinge choices"><label><input type="radio" name="hinge" value="1"> On the side facing the torch</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge" value="2"> On the side facing away from the torch</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge" value="3"> Behind the Moon</label><label><input type="radio" name="hinge" value="4"> Where the torch has moved to</label></div><p id="hinge-feedback" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><p class="small">All answer: finger-vote, board, card, thumbs or point. Your teacher chooses the next step.</p></div><p data-mbm-guide>Use an all-pupil hinge response and the decision rule in the note.</p></section><section class="stage" id="independent"><h2>Independent · 8 min</h2><p>Show day and night at the dot. Explain why the Sun seems to move, even when our torch stays still.</p><p><strong>Show it your way:</strong> Point, sign, speak, draw, type or write. A scribe can help. Staff can take a photo of what you show.</p><p><strong>You can plan in any language.</strong> Say your idea first, then write or draw it if you choose.</p><div class="tier-grid"><div class="tier"><h3>F · First step</h3><p>Point to the lit side and the dark side.</p><p class="small"><strong>Say:</strong> I point to ___ for day and ___ for night.</p></div><div class="tier"><h3>M · Make the link</h3><p>Explain what the dot sees as Earth turns.</p><p class="small"><strong>Say:</strong> The dot has night when ___ because ___.</p></div><div class="tier"><h3>H · Harder thinking</h3><p>Explain why the Sun seems to move across our sky while the model torch stays still.</p><p class="small"><strong>Say:</strong> The Sun seems to move because ___. The model shows ___.</p></div></div><div class="stretch"><strong>Go deeper:</strong> Mark two dots on opposite sides. Explain why one has day while the other has night.</div><p class="small"><strong>Stretch · Two dots, one turn:</strong> Two dots, one turn: test the claim that the Sun goes round Earth each day. Show the motion that explains both dots. Name one limit of the torch model.</p><p data-mbm-guide>One adult supports agreed pupils, the other holds the group; swap across the week. One-minute reset at end.</p></section><section class="stage" id="review"><h2>Review · 4 min</h2><p>Tell a partner what your model shows. Hear one comment. Change one part and say what you changed.</p><p><strong>My feedback was…</strong> to check which way the dot faces.</p><p><strong>Try a fix:</strong> turn the dot or change one phrase (//). Ask “Read this back to me” (?) if that helps.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Give one specific comment, invite a self-edit (//) or read-back (?).</p></section><section class="stage" id="exit"><h2>Exit · 3 min</h2><p>Show how you know. Tell us your feedback and next step. Say, point, sign, draw or write.</p><h3>Check each goal</h3><ul><li>I can point to the day and night sides.</li><li>I can turn the model and explain when night comes.</li><li>I can explain why the Sun seems to move.</li></ul><p><strong>How I know:</strong> I can turn the model and explain when one place has day and night.</p><p><strong>NS(+) · Next I need to:</strong> check the dot before I name day or night.</p><p><strong>Who hears it:</strong> Your teacher or TA sees or hears your answer. They use it to choose the next model or help.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Hear answer, choose next model or help; hand over highest prompt and independent part.</p></section></main><footer>GROW Science · Week 1</footer><script>const calm=document.getElementById('calm');calm.addEventListener('click',()=>{const on=document.body.classList.toggle('calm');calm.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(on));calm.textContent=on?'Standard view':'Calm view'});const check=document.getElementById('faded-check');const choice=document.getElementById('faded-choice');const out=document.getElementById('faded-feedback');check.addEventListener('click',()=>{out.textContent=choice.value==='night'?'Yes. The dot faces away from light, so it is night.':choice.value==='day'?'Look at the dot: it now faces away from light. Try again.':'Choose day or night, then check.'});document.querySelectorAll('input[name="hinge"]').forEach(x=>x.addEventListener('change',()=>{document.getElementById('hinge-feedback').textContent='Choice marked. Show your answer.'}));</script></body></html>
