-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Investigating a science question · GROW Science Autumn 2 W6</title><style>:root{font-family:Arial,Verdana,sans-serif;color:#173039;background:#ecf2f3;line-height:1.55}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0}a{color:#0d536a}button,select,input{font:inherit}button{cursor:pointer;border:2px solid #21566b;background:#fff;color:#173746;border-radius:.35rem;padding:.4rem .65rem}:focus-visible{outline:4px solid #aa5b00;outline-offset:2px}.skip{position:absolute;left:-9999px}.skip:focus{left:.5rem;top:.5rem;background:#fff;z-index:2}header,main,footer{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:1rem}.hero{background:#143e52;color:#fff}.hero>a:first-child{color:inherit;display:inline-block}.hero h1{font-size:clamp(1.8rem,4vw,2.5rem);line-height:1.2;margin:.3rem 0}.hero p{margin:.35rem 0}.toolbar{display:flex;gap:.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:.6rem max(1rem,calc((100vw - 1120px)/2));background:#fff;border-bottom:2px solid #537687}.stage{background:#fff;border:2px solid #a0b9c0;border-left:7px solid #246b7c;border-radius:.5rem;padding:1rem;margin:1rem 0;scroll-margin-top:.5rem}.stage h2{font-size:1.5rem;margin:0 0 .5rem}.model,.support,.stretch{padding:.7rem;border-radius:.4rem}.model,.support{background:#e6f2f8}.stretch{background:#e5f4e8}.options,.controls{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.65rem}.options label,.tiers>div{display:block;flex:1 1 12rem;border:1px solid #8ba9b2;border-radius:.35rem;padding:.55rem}.tiers{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.65rem}.tiers>div{background:#f2f7f8}.cards{padding:0;list-style:none}.cards li{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;padding:.55rem;border-bottom:1px solid #9bb4bb}.cards span{flex:1 1 12rem}.cards select{max-width:100%;min-width:11rem}.status{font-weight:bold}table{border-collapse:collapse;max-width:100%;margin:.7rem 0}th,td{border:1px solid #718d96;padding:.4rem}svg{max-width:100%;height:auto}figcaption{font-size:.95rem}figure{margin:1rem 0}[data-mbm-guide]{display:none}.calm .hero{background:#475c65}.calm .stage{border-left-color:#6b8390}@media(max-width:600px){header,main,footer{padding:.65rem}.toolbar{padding:.55rem}.tiers{grid-template-columns:1fr}.cards label{width:100%}table{display:block;overflow-x:auto}th,td{padding:.25rem}}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation:none!important;transition:none!important}}@media print{@page{size:A4;margin:12mm}body{background:#fff;color:#000;font-size:10pt}.hero{background:#fff;color:#000}.hero>a,.toolbar,.controls,.skip{display:none!important}.stage{border:0;border-top:1px solid #333;margin:0;padding:.4rem 0;break-inside:avoid}.stage h2{font-size:12pt}.tiers{display:block}.tiers>div{border:0;padding:.1rem}select,input{display:none}}</style></head><body><header class="hero"><a href="../../../index.html">← Lessons</a><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to lesson</a><p>GROW · Science · Autumn 2 · Week 6</p><h1>Investigating a science question</h1><p><strong>I am learning to…</strong> plan a fair investigation, record a result and explain a limit.</p><p>40 minutes</p></header><nav class="toolbar" aria-label="Lesson stages"><a href="#arrival">Arrival</a><a href="#we_do">Together</a><a href="#independent">My turn</a><a href="#exit">Exit</a><button type="button" id="calm" aria-pressed="false">Calm view off</button><button type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print</button></nav><main id="main"><p data-mbm-guide>Verbatim SoW objective: Consolidate and revisit key ideas through investigation.</p><section class="stage" id="arrival"><h2>Arrival · 5 min</h2><p>Choose the question card: does surface change how far this toy goes? Point to what we will change.</p><p data-mbm-guide>Offer a predictable seat or standing spot and the first visual cue. No public test.</p></section><section class="stage" id="starter"><h2>Starter · 3 min</h2><p>Think back. Say, point or write one answer for each question.</p><ol><li>What should match a science claim?</li><li>Name an effect of added greenhouse gases.</li><li>What do we keep the same in a fair test?</li></ol><p>Today I will check:</p><ul><li>I can name what I change and what I keep the same.</li><li>I can record what happened in a small test.</li><li>I can explain one way to make the test more dependable.</li></ul><p data-mbm-guide>Ask three low-stakes retrieval questions; prior coverage must be checked.</p></section><section class="stage" id="i_do"><h2>I do · 5 min</h2><p>I mark a start line. I keep the toy and launch the same. I change the surface, measure distance and repeat. Runs may still vary.</p><p><strong>Words:</strong> variable · fair test · result · repeat</p><table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>It means</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">variable</th><td>a factor that can change in a test</td><td>The surface is our changed variable.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">fair test</th><td>a test that changes one key factor and keeps other key factors the same</td><td>We use the same toy in both runs for a fair test.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">result</th><td>what you observe or measure in a test</td><td>Our result is the distance the toy travelled.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">repeat</th><td>do the test again under the same conditions</td><td>We repeat each run to check our result.</td></tr></tbody></table><figure><svg id="fair-test-diagram" role="img" aria-label="The same toy begins at the same dashed start line on two test tracks. Only the rough or smooth surface changes. Distance arrows show the result to measure; illustrated end marks are examples, not predicted measurements." viewBox="0 0 640 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title>A fair test of toy travel on two surfaces</title>
